There is a dead fly in the bathroom sink.

Every morning I find it there, an inch away from the drain. Never in a different spot, never in a different pose.

For weeks, each day it would be the same thing: find the fly, flush the fly, wash the sink. And yet at the start of each new day, there was the fly.

At first, logic tried to solve the mystery. The act of flushing the fly each day ensured that my psyche would remain unharmed by its peculiarity. It seemed so obvious that somewhere I had some rotten bag I'd forgotten about, and in such a bag was the birthing grounds that created these daily obstacles in my sink.

Nevermind that I never saw flies anywhere.

Nevermind that I tore up the house twice within two months trying to unveil the mystery of where these flies kept coming from.

There seemed to be no sensible explanation. Like a fucking fly, on a time loop, trapped in my sink.

This thought began to haunt me.

Its the same fly.

And while this thought haunted me without mercy, I did nothing to prove it true, or otherwise, for quite some time. In fact, I cannot say what took me so long to make a different choice at all. Every morning became a new routine: I would spend 10 minutes in crisis, certain it was the SAME FLY, before flying him away, and scrubbing the sink with a growning intensity that most would label easily as insanity.

One morning, I chose not to flush him. I am not sure why it took so many mornings to reach a day in which I would make such a choice, except to suggest that my mind was not yet ready to